#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168050 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x \\in A$ and $0$ if $x \\notin A$.\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]$.\n\nIn this lesson, we introduce a more general notation that allows us to write indicator functions for any condition, not just set membership.\n\nThe **Iverson bracket notation** $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ takes a predicate $P$ and returns\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is true, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is false$.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[3 > 2] = 1$ because $3 > 2$ is true.\n- $\\mathbf{1}[5 \\leq 4] = 0$ because $5 \\leq 4$ is false.\n- $\\mathbf{1}[7 \\in \\{2, 5, 7\\}] = 1$ because $7$ is an element of $\\{2, 5, 7\\}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "83dcb8a1-56ef-59d0-addc-dfaa18628695" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14816 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168051 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions are useful for writing expressions that behave differently depending on whether a condition is met.\n\n**Sums with indicator functions.** When we multiply a term by an indicator function inside a sum, the indicator acts as a filter. For example, given values $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, the sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0] \\cdot x_i\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values. If $x_i > 0$, the indicator equals $1$ and the term contributes $x_i$. If\n\n$$\nx_i \\leq 0\n$$\n\nthe indicator equals $0$ and the term contributes nothing.\n\nConsider the values\n\n$$\nx_1 = 5, x_2 = -3, x_3 = 2\n$$\n\nUsing the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(3)1[x_{i} > 0] \\cdot x_{i} &= 1[5 > 0] \\cdot 5 + 1[- 3 > 0] \\cdot (-3) + 1[2 > 0] \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 5 + 0 \\cdot (-3) + 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 5 + 0 + 2 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0]\n$$\n\ncounts the positive values. For this data, the count is\n\n$$\n1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc59a9ec-2473-5942-9ab7-f81210d7426c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14817 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Sums"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168059 :tutorial/content "The set-based notation from the previous lesson is a special case of Iverson bracket notation. Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in A]\n$$\n\nBoth expressions equal $1$ when $x \\in A$ and $0$ when $x \\notin A$.\n\nThe Iverson bracket is more flexible because it works with any predicate, such as inequalities. For example:\n\n$$\n{1\\begin{vmatrix}ifx < 0 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}ifx \\ge 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a719d20-4e48-50a9-9e39-db7296bd47d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14942 :tutorial/title "Set Notation and Bracket Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168060 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions can also express piecewise functions compactly. Consider the absolute value function:\n\n$$\n{x,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\geq 0[/math], \\\\ -x,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x < 0[/math]\n$$\n\nWe can write this as a single expression using indicator functions:\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 0] \\cdot x + \\mathbf{1}[x < 0] \\cdot (-x)\n$$\n\nSince the conditions\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand $x < 0$ are mutually exclusive, exactly one indicator equals $1$ and the other equals $0$. This \"switch\" mechanism selects the appropriate term automatically.\n\nLet's verify this expression for a negative number, say $x = -4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid - 4 \\mid &= 1[- 4 \\ge 0] \\cdot (-4) + 1[- 4 < 0] \\cdot (- (-4)) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot (-4) + 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50ebf85-232e-5ea7-aed0-188bbb2467bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14963 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Piecewise Functions"}]}
